  {
   "id": "EybPV8iNBgAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-07-30T19:01:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] read_stan_csv and Variational Inference Samples",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "We&#39;re going to rewrite these so that it works.  This\r<br>was bad on our part for writing such brittle CSV readers.\r<br>They should just read the CSV bits, not try to scan in\r<br>metadata, jump fixed numbers of lines ahead, etc. etc.\r<br>All way too brittle.  It should be better after the refactor,\r<br>but there&#39;s no time frame on this at least until we deal\r<br>with all the compiler compatiblity issues we&#39;re currently having.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Jul 30, 2015, at 12:20 PM, Ben Goodrich &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">goodri...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On Thursday, July 30, 2015 at 11:52:56 AM UTC-4, Cody Ross wrote:\r<br>&gt; Thanks for the heads up on this.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Doesn&#39;t the cmdstan guide say that the VarInf and MCMC output follow almost the same form? They both are just samples of parameters right?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; What is the difference in their structure?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Not much but enough to trip up stan_read_csv(), mostly in the metadata I think.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Ben\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
